\begin_layout Chapter
What is Snapshot Manager?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quotation
\begin_inset Quotes bld
\end_inset

The Snapshot Manager is currently the most distant,
 and least certain aspect of the proposal:
 many decisions can be deferred until other parts of the work are complete,
 including the choice of implementation language.
\begin_inset Quotes brd
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\size scriptsize
—
 Bryn M.
 Reeves,
 Boot-to-snapshot design v0.6,
 2017.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Snapshot Manager,
 aka 
\begin_inset Quotes bld
\end_inset


\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
snapm
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Quotes brd
\end_inset

 is a snapshot manager for Linux systems.
 It orchestrates storage operations for common storage stacks including LVM2 and Stratis and simplifies snapshot creation,
 management,
 and recovery of system and data volumes.
 It grew out of earlier work on bootable snapshots for Linux systems and is supported on recent versions of Fedora,
 CentOS Stream,
 and Red Hat Enterprise Linux.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Central to the concept of 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
snapm
\end_layout

\end_inset

 is the idea of Snapshot Sets —
 named collections of snapshots of different volumes taken at the same moment in time and managed as a single administrative unit.
 The tool uses a plugin architecture to support different kinds of snapshot capable storage.
 This manual aims to describe the tool and its use,
 as well as common scenarios where readers may find it useful.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Snapshot Manager is small,
 but it fills a gap.
 Linux has had snapshot capabilities for years - LVM2,
 Stratis,
 device-mapper - but they're fragmented and complex.
 We provide a single interface that makes them actually usable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A caveat before we begin:
 snapshots are not backups.
 They live on the same storage as your data,
 and if that storage fails,
 your snapshots fail with it.
 Nothing in this book replaces a tested backup strategy.
 But for the everyday mishaps —
 the botched update,
 the configuration mistake,
 the accidental deletion —
 snapshots will save you more times than you can count.
 Just remember:
 backups will get you through times of no snapshots better than snapshots will get you through times of no backups.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
One final note —
 we do not seek to present snapshots as a panacea.
 We think that they are an excellent solution to many common problems,
 but your mileage may vary.
 Consider them one more option in today's ever expanding systems management toolbox.
 Depending on your particular situation and the needs you have identified,
 Snapshot Manager may not be the solution you need right now,
 and that is absolutely fine:
 we promise not to take it personally!
\end_layout

