 Boot Manager (2017)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In 2017 the author was asked to lead a small team looking into the possibility of enabling easy snapshot boot support for Linux,
 and to explore the possibility of automating the use of snapshots to provide a safety net for system upgrades (
\begin_inset Quotes bld
\end_inset

rollback
\begin_inset Quotes brd
\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
That work ultimately led to the white paper 
\begin_inset Quotes bld
\end_inset

Boot-to-snapshot design v0.6
\begin_inset Quotes brd
\end_inset

,
 published on the 8th June 2017.
 This,
 in turn,
 led to the development of the Boom Boot Manager (aka 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
boom-boot
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 aka 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
boom(8)
\end_layout

\end_inset

),
 which is now up to version 1.6.8 and has been in production use and support since early 2018.
 The original white paper is still available in our GitHub repositories,
 although its content is primarily of historical interest today:
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Flex URL
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

https://github.com/snapshotmanager/snapshot-boot-docs/blob/main/docs/design/snapshot-boot-design.pdf
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
boom
\end_layout

\end_inset

 command is a simple template driven configuration utility for creating boot loader entries according to the 
\begin_inset Flex Emph
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Boot Loader Specification
\end_layout

\end_inset

 (BLS) from the 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
systemd
\end_layout

\end_inset

 UAPI group:
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Flex URL
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

https://uapi-group.org/specifications/specs/boot_loader_specification/
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It facilitates the creation and maintenance of auxiliary boot entries on systems that use a BLS compliant boot loader (currently GNU GRUB2 as patched in Fedora,
 CentOS Stream,
 and Red Hat Enterprise Linux,
 and some other distributions,
 IBM zSeries zIPL,
 and 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
systemd-boot(7)
\end_layout

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Although 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
boom
\end_layout

\end_inset

 was developed from the beginning with snapshot boot in mind it also serves other niche purposes that are not always well accommodated by existing tools:
 configuring debug boot entries,
 managing alternate boot environments (different root devices,
 different boot images,
 different kernel command line options etc.).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
While boom did enable snapshot boot for very simple system configurations (systems with just a single root file system mount - no separate 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
/home
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
/var
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 etc.) it was limited in its scope due to the lack of system wide support for multi volume snapshot boot and did not address any of the higher level snapshot management needs identified in the white paper.
\end_layout

