\begin_layout Chapter
Your First Snapshot Set
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quotation
\begin_inset Quotes bld
\end_inset

My little baby sister can do it with ease.
 It's easier than learning your ABCs
\begin_inset Quotes brd
\end_inset


\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\size scriptsize
—
 Kylie Minogue,
 
\begin_inset Quotes bld
\end_inset

The Locomotion
\begin_inset Quotes brd
\end_inset

,
 1988
\size default

\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Kylie's optimism regarding ease of use was unfortunately not reviewed against the real world scenarios of enterprise storage array crashes,
 metadata exhaustion,
 and drive failure.
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A snapshot set is a collection of snapshots,
 bound together with a name,
 and created at a common moment in time
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The snapshots making up a set are created 
\begin_inset Flex Emph
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
sequentially
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 since there is presently no interface in Linux for creating multiple snapshots simultaneously.
 For this reason the individual snapshots will all have very slightly different timestamps.
 Snapshot Manager quantises these timestamps to provide a single unambiguous creation time for the set as a whole.
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 At a bare minimum,
 to create one,
 you just need a name and a list of sources (
\begin_inset Flex Emph
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
mount points
\end_layout

\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Flex Emph
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
block devices
\end_layout

\end_inset

) that you would like to include in the set.
 More advanced options exist to give additional control over the creation process and the resulting snapshot set:
 we will cover these in detail later on in this manual.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Choosing Sources:
 First Things to know.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Before you can create your first snapshot set you'll need to ensure that you have everything necessary in place:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
You need snapshot capable storage:
 LVM2 regular or thin provisioned volumes,
 or Stratis file systems.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
You need free space for your snapshots to use (either in an LVM2 volume group with regular volumes,
 or in the thin pool containing the volumes with LVM2 Thin provisioning or Stratis storage).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
You'll need to know the 
\begin_inset Flex Emph
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
mount point
\end_layout

\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Flex Emph
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
block device path
\end_layout

\end_inset

 for the volumes you wish to include in your snapshot set.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
You need 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
snapm
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For your first experiment,
 it doesn't matter what you snapshot as long as it meets these criteria.
 Later,
 when you are using the tool in anger,
 you can craft specific commands for different use cases or automate the process via schedules (see chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "chap:The-Scheduler"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

).
 To get the most from this exercise,
 it is helpful to be able to make some changes to whatever you snapshot so you'll have genuine differences to examine.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Sources for Snapshot Manager can be either 
\begin_inset Flex Emph
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
mount points
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 or 
\begin_inset Flex Emph
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
block devices
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 A mount point source corresponds to a mounted file system.
 You can specify it either by mount point path (for example,
 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
/home
\end_layout

\end_inset

) or by the underlying block device path (for example,
 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
/dev/vg0/home
\end_layout

\end_inset

).
 A block device source is a block device that is not mounted and that need not contain a mountable file system image (for example,
 databases,
 virtual machine images,
 and other non file system content).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Snapshot Manager uses the mechanism of 
\begin_inset Flex Emph
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
provider plugins
\end_layout

\end_inset

 to support different types of storage.
 A provider plugin corresponds to a particular storage technology (LVM2 CoW,
 LVM2 Thin,
 Stratis,
 and so on).
 The 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
snapm
\end_layout

\end_inset

 command will determine the correct plugins to use for any given source (assuming that one exists and is able to snapshot the requested volume) —
 you do not need to specify which plugins to use on the command line.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Some familiarity with your storage stack's management tools (whether that's 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
vgs(8)
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
lvs(8)
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 
\nospellcheck on
et
\nospellcheck default
 
\nospellcheck on
al
\nospellcheck default
.
 for LVM2,
 or the 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
stratis(8)
\end_layout

\end_inset

 command line tool for Stratis storage) will help when it comes to planning,
 monitoring,
 and finding your way around.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

Knowledge of common Linux administrative tools like the 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
mount(8)
\end_layout

\end_inset

 program,
 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
findmnt(8)
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
lsblk(8)
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 and 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
blkid(8)
\end_layout

\end_inset

 will also be an advantage —
 refer to the respective manual pages for more details,
 or refer to one of the many great Linux systems administration tutorials available on the internet.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Naming Your Snapshot Set
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
You are free to name your snapshot set anything you like within the constraints enforced by 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
snapm
\end_layout

\end_inset

 and its provider plugins:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The 
\begin_inset Quotes bld
\end_inset

_
\begin_inset Quotes brd
\end_inset

 (underscore) character is not permitted.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The valid characters for snapshot set names are the ASCII lowercase and uppercase alphabetic characters,
 the digits,
 and the symbols 
\begin_inset Quotes bld
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Quotes brd
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Quotes bld
\end_inset

+
\begin_inset Quotes brd
\end_inset

,
 and 
\begin_inset Quotes bld
\end_inset


\nospellcheck on
-
\nospellcheck default

\begin_inset Quotes brd
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Certain strings may be forbidden by particular provider plugins:
 for instance,
 LVM2 reserves the string 
\begin_inset Quotes bld
\end_inset

_
\nospellcheck on
mlog
\nospellcheck default

\begin_inset Quotes brd
\end_inset

 (and others) for internal logical volume names (refer to 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
lvm(8)
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
lvmraid(7)
\end_layout

\end_inset

 and related manual pages for more information).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Certain providers may enforce a maximum name length,
 for example,
 for LVM2 volumes this is 127 characters.
 Note that this limit includes parts of the internal name generated by 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
snapm
\end_layout

\end_inset

 (which varies according to the mount point the snapshot refers to),
 so the usable name length is somewhat shorter.
 Nobody likes long device names anyway so this should not be a concern in most situations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Any provider that represents the snapshot name as an entry in the file system (which includes all current and planned snapshot providers) is naturally limited by the maximum allowed file name length on Linux (255 characters).
 Again,
 this absolute maximum is reduced somewhat by the need for 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
snapm
\end_layout

\end_inset

 to encode various information into the names it generates.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
If you include a 
\begin_inset Quotes bld
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Quotes brd
\end_inset

 followed by a nonnegative number at the end of your snapshot set name 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
snapm
\end_layout

\end_inset

 will consider it to be an 
\begin_inset Flex Emph
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
index
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 This is useful when taking recurring snapshots sets of the same things with a common 
\begin_inset Flex Emph
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
base name
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 You can also have 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
snapm
\end_layout

\end_inset

 add one automatically using the 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
--autoindex
\end_layout

\end_inset

 option (more on that later).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
it is generally best to not include a date or time reference in the name unless you really,
 really want to:
 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
snapm
\end_layout

\end_inset

 already stores the creation time of the snapshot set as a UNIX epoch value and renders it as human readable date and time strings in the local time zone.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The name 
\begin_inset Quotes bld
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Quotes brd
\end_inset

 is reserved by 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
snapm
\end_layout

\end_inset

 for referencing the live root file system.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Aim for memorable and meaningful names:
 
\begin_inset Quotes bld
\end_inset


\nospellcheck on
quux-foo-backup
\nospellcheck default

\begin_inset Quotes brd
\end_inset

 might seem like a good idea at the time,
 when you're typing in a hurry,
 but future you will thank present you for coming up with something more self-explanatory.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Good names might be:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
before-upgrade
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
after-upgrade
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
pre-deployment
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
post-deployment
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
hourly.N
\end_layout

\end_inset

 (where 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Flex Emph
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
N
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset

 is an integer uniquifying index either appended manually or enabled automatically with 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
--autoindex
\end_layout

\end_inset

 - see 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Schedules-and-Autoindex"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Bad names would include:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
backup
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
thing
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
thisone
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
thatone
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
something-something-something-snapshot-side
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
foo
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
fubar
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
plugh
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
quux
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
thud
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
xyzzy
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
qwerty
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 etc.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Overriding The Default Size Policy
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Snapshot Manager provides 
\begin_inset Flex Emph
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
size policies
\end_layout

\end_inset

 that allow the user to request that a certain amount of space be available when creating snapshots.
 For some providers (LVM2 Thin,
 Stratis) this is simply a check that this space is available at the time the snapshot set is created.
 For LVM2 CoW it determines the actual size of the snapshot exception store that the tool will allocate.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The default size policy is different depending on whether you are creating a snapshot from a mount point source or a block device source.
 For mount points the default size policy specifies twice the space currently used on the mount point (
\begin_inset Quotes bld
\end_inset


\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
200%USED
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Quotes brd
\end_inset

 in size policy notation).
