\begin_layout Section
Foundational Concepts
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This section defines some terms that we will use extensively in this manual.
 Our definitions may not always agree with other explanations you may come across,
 but within this book these are the meanings that we attach to these words.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Block Devices as an Address Space / Array of Blocks
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
An outdated view of 
\begin_inset Quotes bld
\end_inset

what is a block device
\begin_inset Quotes brd
\end_inset

 might state that a block device is an electromechanical assembly that persists data to a physical medium and provides a control interface for reading and writing fixed-sized blocks of data (OK,
 that is more precisely a description of some form of 
\begin_inset Flex Emph
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
disk drive
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 but let us run with this for a moment —
 for the longest time that was pretty much the only game in town when it came to block devices).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A slightly less anachronistic definition would describe a peripheral device containing a small computer that manages a pool of storage media with its own internal algorithms,
 presenting an abstract IO interface to the host system via fixed size data blocks.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
While there are elements of truth in both these formulations we advocate a more abstract viewpoint when thinking about snapshots.
 Fundamentally,
 a block device is an abstraction that presents the device to the operating system and the user as an 
\begin_inset Flex Emph
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
address space
\end_layout

\end_inset

:
 an ordered sequence of locations that can be read from or written to in fixed sized blocks.
 The operating system takes care of buffering data being read or written and provides a uniform interface to the user and especially to file systems that may reside on the device.
 This is similar conceptually to the memory (RAM) of a computer,
 which is an address space that can be read from or written to in units of bytes (or sometimes 
\begin_inset Flex Emph
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
machine words
\end_layout

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Historically the size of the blocks was often 512 bytes but now may commonly be 4096 bytes (4KiB) or larger —
 this is neither here nor there for our present purpose (although in practical systems administration it can be a very important consideration when creating storage configurations and file systems).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This abstraction sits well with the UNIX approach that 
\begin_inset Quotes bld
\end_inset

everything is a file
\begin_inset Quotes brd
\end_inset


\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Flex URL
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Everything_is_a_file
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset

 and indeed has remained unchanged since the very early days of UNIX and 
\nospellcheck on
UNIX-like
\nospellcheck default
 systems (ignoring messy details like 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
ioctl
\end_layout

\end_inset

 commands,
 IO scheduling,
 and actual hardware).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:The-Concept-of"

\end_inset

The Concept of an Image
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
An 
\begin_inset Flex Emph
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
image
\end_layout

\end_inset

 then,
 is the state of the contents of the device address space at some specific point in time:
 for example,
 a 
\begin_inset Flex Emph
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
backup image
\end_layout

\end_inset

 is a serialised copy of the device content at the moment the backup was taken.
 When we create snapshots of block devices using technology like the Linux kernel's device-mapper we are preserving an 
\begin_inset Flex Emph
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
image
\end_layout

\end_inset

 of the device as it existed at the moment the snapshot was created.
 This is very useful for a number of purposes:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Creating an image to be written to backup media (tape,
 archival servers and so on)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Preserving a copy of the system before making potentially risky changes (software updates,
 configuration changes,
 data edits)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Taking a copy of the live data to use with tools that might cause damage (file system editors,
 file system repair) or that require stable data to function correctly (forensic tools,
 read only file system checks)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Quotes bld
\end_inset

Forking
\begin_inset Quotes brd
\end_inset

 the system into multiple parallel versions if itself (creating multiple virtual machine images from a common base image,
 preparing test cases)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
One property of an image that is relevant to our interests is the notion of 
\begin_inset Flex Emph
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
consistency
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 Data that is consistent is uniform,
 accurate,
 and reliable across its layers (physical and conceptual).
 There are different types of consistency that we can talk about in relation to the 
\begin_inset Flex Emph
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
images
\end_layout

\end_inset

 that we describe here:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Physical consistency and integrity —
 is the media intact?
 Can the address space be read without errors or missing portions?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
File system consistency —
 is the image a valid file system that is consistent in its internal metadata?
 Does it pass 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
fsck(8)
\end_layout

\end_inset

 and can it be mounted by the kernel using the 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
mount(8)
\end_layout

\end_inset

 program?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
User data consistency —
 is the data stored within the file system in an internally consistent state?
 Does it reflect the intended content at the time of the last write?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Application consistency —
 does the data across multiple files (possibly in multiple file systems) represent a coherent and consistent state of the ultimate application or applications running on the system?
 Can it be used to restart operations following a service disruption such as a crash,
 power outage,
 or hardware failure.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Block Device Snapshots
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now that we have defined a 
\begin_inset Flex Emph
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
block device
\end_layout

\end_inset

 and an 
\begin_inset Flex Emph
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
image
\end_layout

\end_inset

 the idea of a block device 
\begin_inset Flex Emph
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
snapshot
\end_layout

\end_inset

 flows naturally from that foundation:
 a block device snapshot is just a means to capture an image of a block device at a particular moment in time.
 As ever,
 there are many roads to Rome:
 the Linux device-mapper,
 external storage arrays,
 even traditional techniques like 
\begin_inset Quotes bld
\end_inset

split mirrors
\begin_inset Quotes brd
\end_inset

 (isolating one or more members of a mirrored RAID volume to obtain and preserve an image).
 When it comes to block device snapshots,
 in this manual we will focus almost entirely on the Linux device-mapper based solutions:
 LVM2 CoW,
 LVM2 Thin and Stratis.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
How File System Snapshots Fit Into This
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the case of modern file system snapshots (Btrfs,
 bcachefs,
 ZFS,
 and others) there is no literal image as exists in a 
\begin_inset Flex Emph
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
block device
\end_layout

\end_inset

 snapshot.
 Rather,
 the image is notional or abstract and maintained internally by the file system within its metadata:
 different file systems adopt different approaches to maintaining this illusion but they share the common property that they present the resulting file system state to the user in ways that allow the content to be accessed in the state it was in at the moment the snapshot was created.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Commonly this is achieved by 
\begin_inset Flex Emph
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
mounting
\end_layout

\end_inset

 the snapshot volume somewhere.
 In other cases the snapshot may appear similar to a regular directory within the file system
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
In at least one case,
 it is virtually identical and cannot be readily distinguished from an ordinary directory:
 we are not fond of this approach since it makes life difficult for tools that want to manage snapshots automatically,
 and that is after all what we are about.
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
On The 
\begin_inset Flex Emph
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Origin
\end_layout

\end_inset

 of Snapshots
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We will use the term 
\begin_inset Flex Emph
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
origin
\end_layout

\end_inset

 frequently in this manual.
 In simple terms the origin of a snapshot is the volume from which the snapshot was taken.
 The snapshot is a faithful copy of the origin at the moment in time at which it was taken.
 In some cases (LVM2 CoW) the origin exists and is accessible at all times at which the snapshot exists:
 the snapshot depends on its original —
 in a very real,
 and physical way.
 In other cases (LVM2 Thin,
 Stratis) it is possible for the origin to be deleted or otherwise cease to exist independently of its snapshots (see chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "chap:Storage-Back-ends"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 for more details on precisely how and why this can occur,
 and what to do about it).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This can create a rather confusing situation:
 the snapshot has lost its real world referent,
 but from a data consistency standpoint nothing unusual is happening here.
 The snapshot continues to preserve the state of the origin as it was at the moment of its creation (modulo any subsequent modifications to the snapshot in the common case of writable snapshots).
 It is arguable that at this point it is no longer a snapshot,
 but rather has become 
\begin_inset Flex Emph
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Just Another Volume
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 but this is of little importance to us,
 save for where the tools limit our options as a result.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Ontological Status - Is the Snapshot a 
\begin_inset Quotes bld
\end_inset

Copy
\begin_inset Quotes brd
\end_inset

 Or is it Equally 
\begin_inset Quotes bld
\end_inset

Real
\begin_inset Quotes brd
\end_inset

?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The snapshot is an image.
 It is a representation of the data on the origin at some moment in time,
 with or without further changes.
 In this sense it is equally as real as the volume from which it was created:
 it can be opened,
 read,
 possibly written,
 and mounted assuming that it contains a usable file system image.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Temporal Primality - Which System is 
\begin_inset Quotes bld
\end_inset

THE
\begin_inset Quotes brd
\end_inset

 System When Both Exist?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This is a difficult question and there exists more than one possible interpretation.
 We suggest that it is most useful to consider whatever system is currently 
\begin_inset Flex Emph
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
booted
\end_layout

\end_inset

 as the 
\begin_inset Quotes bld
\end_inset

real
\begin_inset Quotes bld
\end_inset

 system in any given moment:
 that is what is currently running,
 and it is where changes made to the system by the administrator or programs that are running will naturally end up.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If you boot into a snapshot then,
 for the time being at least,
 the snapshot becomes 
\begin_inset Flex Strong
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
the
\end_layout

\end_inset

 system.
 The software that runs will be the version present in the snapshot image and changes that are written while the snapshot is in use will modify the snapshot rather than the origin devices that represent the system's normal environment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
All that being said,
 it is still important to remember where you are at any given moment and to act accordingly:
 this is no different than the ordinary situational awareness that the administrator must maintain.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This has important consequences in the case of updates,
 and particularly for the security of the system (since snapshots may contain old versions of software that include vulnerabilities).
 See chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "chap:Security,-Privacy,-and"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 for a more complete discussion of these concerns.
 Don't get lost in your snapshots.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Action Location - Does it Matter WHERE You Perform the Upgrade?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Practical considerations first:
 Snapshot Manager does not care whether you upgrade in the live root file system or in a snapshot set,
 but upgrade tools might.
 Running 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
dnf update
\end_layout

\end_inset

 in a mounted snapshot set is generally pretty safe,
 but watch out for kernel updates that will try to create boot entries for kernels that may not exist in the main root file system.
 This may prove confusing so we generally advise against it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
More complicated tools like installer based upgrades,
 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
dnf system-upgrade
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 and the Leapp application and OS modernisation framework are generally not designed with upgrading in snapshots in mind:
 
\begin_inset Flex Emph
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
caveat emptor
\end_layout

\end_inset

 and do not attempt this on a production system without thoroughly testing your intended upgrade process in a safe environment first.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For now,
 we suggest following convention unless you are certain about the behaviour of the tools you are using.
 Upgrade the system,
 and keep a snapshot set around as your escape hatch.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Operation Semantics - 
\begin_inset Quotes bld
\end_inset

Revert
\begin_inset Quotes brd
\end_inset

 Implies Hierarchy;
 
\begin_inset Quotes bld
\end_inset

Merge
\begin_inset Quotes brd
\end_inset

 Implies Equality
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
snapm
\end_layout

\end_inset

 command uses the subcommand verb 
\begin_inset Quotes bld
\end_inset


\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
revert
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Quotes brd
\end_inset

 to request applying the snapshot content to its origin volume and 
\begin_inset Quotes bld
\end_inset

rolling back
\begin_inset Quotes brd
\end_inset

 the system to its original state.
 The LVM2 tools use the term 
\begin_inset Flex Emph
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
merge
\end_layout

\end_inset

 for the same operation.
 This recognises the fact that in practice there is no enforced hierarchy and the snapshot may be the updated content,
 relative to its origin volume.
 We think that 
\begin_inset Quotes bld
\end_inset


\begin_inset Flex Emph
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
revert
\begin_inset Quotes brd
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset

 is conceptually simpler and in keeping with our current advice to perform upgrades on the main system volumes rather than inside snapshots.
\end_layout

