status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The 
\begin_inset Quotes bld
\end_inset

a ship in a harbour is safe
\begin_inset Quotes brd
\end_inset

 adage was originally coined by John A.
 Shedd in 
\begin_inset Quotes bld
\end_inset

Salt from my Attic
\begin_inset Quotes brd
\end_inset

,
 1928.
 Hopper expanded and popularised the phrase in computing contexts throughout her career.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The general topic of storage planning is vast and encompasses the full gamut of tasks from capacity planning and usage estimation,
 through physical layer and fabric concerns,
 to file system creation micro-optimisations and mount options.
 It is not our objective in this chapter to try to describe all these disparate activities in detail:
 rather,
 our aim is to spell out the practical considerations that arise when including 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
snapm
\end_layout

\end_inset

 deployment and use in the planning scope.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Consult the many fine online resources available on the topic for a broader view of the subject matter,
 or raise the matter with your storage or operating system vendor's team of consultants,
 who will be happy to help inform you of the state of the art (for a modest fee,
 of course).
\end_layout

