\begin_layout Chapter
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "chap:Crash-Consistency-vs."

\end_inset

Crash Consistency vs.
 Application Consistency
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quotation
\begin_inset Quotes bld
\end_inset


\begin_inset Quotes bls
\end_inset

The breath of heaven is out of harmony,
 the breath of earth tangles and snarls,
\begin_inset Quotes brs
\end_inset

 said Cloud Chief.
 
\begin_inset Quotes bls
\end_inset

The six breaths do not blend properly,
 the four seasons do not stay in order.
 Now I would like to harmonise the essences of the six breaths in order to bring nourishment to all living creatures.
 How should I go about it?
\begin_inset Quotes brs
\end_inset


\begin_inset Quotes brd
\end_inset


\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\size scriptsize
—
 Hong Meng,
 Zhuangzi,
 Ca.
 3C BCE.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We laid out some basic principles of image consistency in section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:The-Concept-of"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

:
 it is now time to expand on that groundwork to understand how to achieve different levels of consistency and how those consistency levels affect the behaviour of our systems and the applications that run on them.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The basic level of consistency achieved by a snapshot taken at an arbitrary moment in time with no special preparation can be described as 
\begin_inset Flex Emph
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
crash consistent
\end_layout

\end_inset

 —
 that is to say,
 the content of the snapshot images is as it would be if the system had crashed at that moment:
 file systems may need to be checked,
 journals may need to be replayed,
 and the application data reflected in those file systems may correspond to some in between state that is not a proper reflection of the application's required invariants.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Linux device-mapper snapshots (both CoW and thin) actually do a little better than this by default:
 they use the equivalent of the Linux kernel 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
FIFREEZE
\end_layout

\end_inset

/
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
FITHAW
\end_layout

\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
ioctl(2)
\end_layout

\end_inset

 commands
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
These commands may be issued manually by the administrator by invoking the 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
fsfreeze(8)
\end_layout

\end_inset

 program.
 Be aware of the potential for deadlock when using these commands —
 particularly if attempting to freeze the root file system of a running machine.
\end_layout

\end_inset

internally.
 The effect of the freeze command is to halt any new access to the file system,
 and to force outstanding IO to the disk (particularly metadata IO) in order to create a stable image on disk.
 This guarantees 
\begin_inset Flex Strong
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
file system consistency
\end_layout

\end_inset

 —
 the snapshot will pass 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
fsck(8)
\end_layout

\end_inset

 and mount cleanly.
 The image may still require journal recovery or other actions when it is later mounted (specifically to clean up 
\begin_inset Flex Emph
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
orphan inodes
\end_layout

\end_inset

 —
 files that were unlinked but in use at the time the snapshot was taken),
 but the internal data structures used by the file system will be in a consistent and predictable state requiring no repair before they can be used.
\end_layout

