\begin_layout Standard
The RPM Package Manager
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
That is its formal name —
 it is a recursive acronym,
 like EMACS.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 provides a powerful verification system that can detect and report unexpected attributes of files managed by installed RPM packages.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Before we begin,
 one note of caution:
 since the 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
rpm(8)
\end_layout

\end_inset

 command normally uses a package database that is stored in the host file system,
 it is vulnerable to corruption and misleading information stored in that database (whether accidental damage caused by users,
 corruption resulting from hardware or software errors,
 or deliberate tampering by malicious actors).
 Stronger results can be obtained by the use of external databases on read only media,
 but,
 for every day state check-ups and confirmation it is still a useful and powerful tool.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The main verification mode of RPM is 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
-V/–-verify
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 This argument can be used with a list of package names,
 or with the special option 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
-a/–all
\end_layout

\end_inset

to request verification of all packages installed on the system.
\end_layout

