\begin_layout Section
Using Snapshot Manager
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Flex Strong
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Q:
\end_layout

\end_inset

 What's the difference between a Snapshot and a Snapshot Set?
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset Flex Strong
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
A:
\end_layout

\end_inset

 A 
\begin_inset Flex Emph
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Snapshot
\end_layout

\end_inset

 is an individual instance of a block device or file system snapshot (managed by 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
snapm
\end_layout

\end_inset

 or not,
 as the case may be).
 A 
\begin_inset Flex Emph
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Snapshot Set
\end_layout

\end_inset

 is a named collection of snapshots taken at the same moment in time and managed by 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
snapm
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset Flex Strong
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Q:
\end_layout

\end_inset

 Can I use the difference engine if I haven't created any snapshot sets yet?
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset Flex Strong
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
A:
\end_layout

\end_inset

 No:
 it is only possible to create difference reports when there is at least one snapshot set to make a comparison against.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset Flex Strong
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Q:
\end_layout

\end_inset

 Do I need to use 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
boom
\end_layout

\end_inset

 in order to use 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
snapm
\end_layout

\end_inset

?
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset Flex Strong
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
A:
\end_layout

\end_inset

 We require 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
boom
\end_layout

\end_inset

 to create bootable snapshot sets but you don't need to interact with it directly (you don't even need to install the 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
boom-boot
\end_layout

\end_inset

 CLI,
 but it can be useful for inspecting your boot entries).
 Unless you have specific requirements the 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
snapm
\end_layout

\end_inset

 tool can take care of all the details for you when you use the 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
--boot
\end_layout

\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
--revert
\end_layout

\end_inset

 options.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Snapshot Set Creation and Management
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Flex Strong
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Q:
\end_layout

\end_inset

 How much space do I need for snapshots?
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset Flex Strong
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
A:
\end_layout

\end_inset

 That is a bit of a 
\begin_inset Quotes bld
\end_inset

how long is a piece of string?
\begin_inset Quotes brd
\end_inset

 question:
 the precise answer depends on the amount of change that occurs to the origin volume or the snapshot during its lifespan.
 Snapshot Manager defaults to allocating twice the space currently used on a mounted file system,
 or half the size of block devices when creating snapshot sets,
 but if you have a better estimate then these values should be adjusted for your particular circumstances.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset Flex Strong
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Q:
\end_layout

\end_inset

 What happens if a snapshot runs out of space?
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset Flex Strong
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
A:
\end_layout

\end_inset

 This will produce errors and may invalidate the snapshot.
 This should be avoided at all costs if you value what the snapshot represents,
 as it will result in data loss.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset Flex Strong
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Q:
\end_layout

\end_inset

 Can I create snapshots of individual files or directories?
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset Flex Strong
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
A:
\end_layout

\end_inset

 No:
 none of the current snapshot providers support snapshots at this granularity.
 A similar result can be obtained on modern file systems via the 
\begin_inset Flex Emph
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
reflink
\end_layout

\end_inset

 facility (see 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
cp(1)
\end_layout

\end_inset

) but these snapshot-like file system objects are not managed by 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
snapm
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset Flex Strong
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Q:
\end_layout

\end_inset

 Can I modify the contents of a snapshot after creation?
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset Flex Strong
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
A:
\end_layout

\end_inset

 Yes.
 Snapshot sets are by default created in read-write mode and can be modified when mounted subject to normal file system permission checks.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset Flex Strong
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Q:
\end_layout

\end_inset

 Why does my snapshot set show as "Reverting" and how long will it take?
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset Flex Strong
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
A:
\end_layout

\end_inset

 This is because a 
\begin_inset Quotes bld
\end_inset


\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
revert
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Quotes brd
\end_inset

 command has been issued (or the underlying storage tools have been used to initiate a revert).
 The time it takes depends on the snapshot provider type:
 in all cases,
 when snapshots or their origin volumes are in use,
 the revert operation is deferred until the next activation (normally at boot time).
 In the case of LVM2 CoW snapshots the underlying 
\begin_inset Flex Emph
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
merge
\end_layout

\end_inset

 operation involves physically moving data:
 this takes time proportional to the amount of data in the snapshot at the time of the merge and the speed of the storage devices involved.
 Thin snapshot reverts (LVM2 Thin and Stratis) are instantaneous at the time the pool is activated.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Scheduling & Clean-up
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Flex Strong
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Q:
\end_layout

\end_inset

 How do I know which garbage collection policy to use?
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset Flex Strong
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
A:
\end_layout

\end_inset

 This depends on how much space you have,
 and for how long you want to keep snapshot sets around.
 The four policies (
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
ALL
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
COUNT
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
AGE
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
TIMELINE
\end_layout

\end_inset

) cover a multitude of possibilities and accept parameters to configure them for your needs.
 See chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "chap:The-Scheduler"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 and chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "chap:Garbage-Collection"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 for a full description of configuring and managing schedules.
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset Flex Strong
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Q:
\end_layout

\end_inset

 Will scheduled snapshots fill up my disk?
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset Flex Strong
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
A:
\end_layout

\end_inset

 Potentially,
 yes.
 The thin provisioned snapshot providers are more space efficient than the LVM2 CoW provider but they will consume space from the pool they are created in (unlike LVM2 CoW which will not exceed the fixed space allocated to it from its containing volume group).
 You should configure a garbage collection policy with parameters that limit space consumption to an acceptable level.
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset Flex Strong
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Q:
\end_layout

\end_inset

 What happens to my snapshots if I delete a schedule?
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset Flex Strong
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
A:
\end_layout

\end_inset

 The snapshot sets will remain until they are manually deleted.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset Flex Strong
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Q:
\end_layout

\end_inset

 Can I manually delete snapshot sets created by a schedule?
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset Flex Strong
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
A:
\end_layout

\end_inset

 Yes:
 use the regular 
\nospellcheck on
'
\nospellcheck default

\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
snapm snapset delete
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Quotes brd
\end_inset

 command with the name or UUID of the snapshot set you want to remove.
 The schedule garbage collection policy will continue to operate as configured.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Boot & Recovery
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Flex Strong
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Q:
\end_layout

\end_inset

 What's the difference between 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
-b/--bootable
\end_layout

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
-r/--revert
\end_layout

\end_inset

?
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset Flex Strong
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
A:
\end_layout

\end_inset

 The 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
-b/--bootable
\end_layout

\end_inset

 option creates a 
\begin_inset Flex Emph
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
bootable
\end_layout

\end_inset

 snapshot set.
 When you select the resulting Snapshot boot entry the snapshot set will boot in place of the normal system environment,
 allowing you to look around and make changes.
 The 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
-r/--revert
\end_layout

\end_inset

 option is used to create a 
\begin_inset Flex Emph
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
fail safe revert
\end_layout

\end_inset

 boot entry.
 This is used in conjunction with the 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
snapm snapset revert
\end_layout

\end_inset

 command to revert the system to the state reflected in the snapshot set.
 The revert boot entry uses backup boot images by default to protect against the case where the original copies have already been removed from the live system.
 See chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "chap:Bootable-Snapshot-Sets"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 for full instructions on using Snapshot Manager's boot integration features.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Flex Strong
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Q:
\end_layout

\end_inset

 Can I boot into a snapshot set without reverting to it?
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset Flex Strong
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
A:
\end_layout

\end_inset

 Yes.
 Select the appropriate Snapshot boot entry from your system's boot menu to boot into the corresponding snapshot set.
 Subsequently,
 when you reboot,
